Q: Font Not Loading in FabricJS Canvas with WebFontLoader (Dynamic Import) A: <h3>Browsers »lazy« font loading</h3>
<p>All modern browsers are highly optimized to load fonts only if really required. This is incredibly useful in regards of complex font loading CSS such as the ones provided by google fonts: These usually include multiple rules for many unicode-ranges. Fortunately, the browser won't load all files for languages that are not needed for the current document.</p>
<p>Simplified the browser behaves something like this:</p>
<ul>
<li>Parse the CSS - any <code>@font-face</code> rules for font loading? OK but I won't load all of them - let's first check the</li>
<li>HTML/DOM – any elements with the specified font-families and styles applied? If not – I don't load anything</li>
<li>dynamic content created via JS? Ah! there's a request for the font - let's load it for the next refresh</li>
</ul>
<p>So the point is: dynamically created elements won't force the browser to wait for the font to be loaded.</p>
<p>You may wonder: »But haven't I applied the font to a text object?«<br />
From a browser perspective – you haven't as the fabric.js text-object doesn't qualify as a DOM element.<br />
While <code>setFontLoaded(true)</code> sets the variable to true it obviously doesn't apply the font to a DOM element.</p>
<p>Here are some working (simplified) examples:</p>
<h4>1. Render after font load</h4>
<p>You current code doesn't work as the rendering doesn't wait for the font load promise to be fulfilled. By running the rendering in the font loader active callback we ensure the font is fully available.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="false" data-babel="true" data-babel-preset-react="true" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>console.clear();
const { useState, useEffect } = React;


function App() {

  useEffect(() =&gt; {

    const fontFamily = 'Rasa';

    // Initialize Fabric canvas
    const canvas = new fabric.Canvas("canvas", {
      width: 800,
      height: 200,
      backgroundColor: "#f0f0f0"
    });

    // Add text to canvas
    const text = new fabric.Text("Hamburgefons", {
      left: 50,
      top: 80,
      fontSize: 72,
      fontFamily: fontFamily,
      fill: "#000"
    });


  WebFont.load({
    google: {
      families: [fontFamily]
    },
    active: ()=&gt;{
      //canvas.fontFamily = fontFamily;
      canvas.add(text);
      canvas.renderAll();
    }
  });

    // Cleanup on unmount
    return () =&gt; canvas.dispose();
  }, []);

  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;canvas id="canvas"&gt;&lt;/canvas&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}

ReactDOM.render(&lt;App /&gt;, document.getElementById("root"));</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script crossorigin src="https://unpkg.com/react@18/umd/react.production.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script crossorigin src="https://unpkg.com/react-dom@18/umd/react-dom.production.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/fabric.js/5.3.0/fabric.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script src="https://ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/webfont/1.5.10/webfont.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;div id="root"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<h4>2. Use native <code>new fontFace()</code> API</h4>
<p>The <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/FontFace" rel="nofollow noreferrer">fontFace API</a> methods don't require a 3rd party font loader but require a explicit font file URL to work.</p>
<p>Related post <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/2608022/how-can-i-use-custom-fonts-in-an-html5-canvas-element/2609867#2609867">&quot;How can I use custom fonts in an HTML5 Canvas element?&quot;</a></p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="true" data-babel-preset-react="true" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const { useState, useEffect } = React;


function App() {
  useEffect(async () =&gt; {
    // Initialize Fabric canvas
    const canvas = new fabric.Canvas("canvas", {
      width: 800,
      height: 200,
      backgroundColor: "#f0f0f0"
    });

    let fontFamily = 'Rasa';
    let url = 'https://fonts.gstatic.com/s/rasa/v27/xn76YHIn1mWmVKl8ZtAM9NrJfN5GJV4yd8o.woff2';

    // Add text to canvas
    const text = new fabric.Text("Hamburgefons", {
      left: 50,
      top: 80,
      fontSize: 72,
      fontFamily: fontFamily,
      fill: "#000"
    });

    let font = new FontFace(`${fontFamily}`, `url(${url})`, {style: "normal",weight: "400"});
    // wait for font to be loaded
    await font.load();
    // add font to document
    document.fonts.add(font);

    canvas.add(text);
    canvas.renderAll();

    // Cleanup on unmount
    return () =&gt; canvas.dispose();
  }, []);

  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;canvas id="canvas"&gt;&lt;/canvas&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}

ReactDOM.render(&lt;App /&gt;, document.getElementById("root"));</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script crossorigin src="https://unpkg.com/react@18/umd/react.production.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script crossorigin src="https://unpkg.com/react-dom@18/umd/react-dom.production.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/fabric.js/5.3.0/fabric.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;div id="root"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<h3>Test with disabled cache</h3>
<p>The behavior in browsers can be deceptive – better run tests with dev tools open an caching disabled (in network tab)</p>
